---
title: Sound effects
description: Make a sound effect from a WAV file, or write a sequence, and play it with a SOUND constant.
sidebar:
  order: 2
---

A sound effect is a file in `audio/sfx/`, and its `SOUND_` constant is named after the file, so `shop_bell.xml` becomes `SOUND_SHOP_BELL`. A sound either plays one sample (a one-shot) or plays several notes in order (a sequence).

## Making a sound effect from a WAV file

{% steps %}
1. In the Filesystem, right-click `audio/sfx` and select **New > Sound Effect**.
2. Choose your WAV file.
3. Enter a name for the sound. Diorama64 suggests one from the WAV file's name. The name can't be one that another sound effect or a sample in any bank already has.
4. Select **OK**.
{% /steps %}

Diorama64 writes `audio/sfx/<name>.xml` as a one-shot that plays your sample. It adds the sample to the first of your mod's sound banks, `SFX1` to `SFX9`, that has room, and creates the bank if it doesn't exist. The sample is converted to mono, and to 32 kHz if its sample rate is higher.

The sample then opens in the Sound editor, where you can trim it and set its tuning and loop. See [Edit an instrument](/papermario-dx/audio/instruments/#editing-an-instrument).

## Changing a one-shot

A one-shot's file looks like this:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SoundEffect>
    <Routing allocation="dynamic" maxPlayer="7" priority="0"/>
    <OneShot wav="shop_bell" volume="70" lockVolume="true" lockPitch="true"/>
</SoundEffect>
```

`OneShot` uses these attributes:

| Attribute | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `wav` | The sample to play. It's the name of an instrument in an always-loaded bank, without `.wav`. To play a sample the game already has, such as `open_popup`, change this name. |
| `volume` | The volume in hexadecimal, from `00` to `7F`. The game squares it, so `40` plays at about a quarter of full volume. |
| `lockVolume`, `lockPitch` | Keep your values when game code plays the sound with its own volume or pitch. `lockPan` and `lockReverb` work the same way. |
| `pan`, `reverb`, `pitch`, `randomPitch`, `envelope` | Optional. If you leave them out, the defaults apply. |

`Routing` decides which of the eight sound effect players can play the sound and how it competes with sounds that are already playing:

| Attribute | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `allocation="dynamic"` | The game picks a player when the sound starts. Use `allocation="fixed"` with a `player` value to always use one player. |
| `maxPlayer` | The highest player number the game can pick, from `0` to `7`. |
| `priority` | From `0` (lowest) to `3` (highest). A sound can replace a playing sound of equal or lower priority. |

If you copy the `Routing` line from a similar sound in the game's `audio/sfx/`, the new sound behaves the same way.

## Writing a sequence

Use a sequence when the sound needs several notes, timing, loops, or more than one track. A sequence has `Tracks` instead of a `OneShot`, and it needs a `Routing` element:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SoundEffect>
    <Routing allocation="dynamic" maxPlayer="7" priority="0"/>
    <Tracks>
        <Track slot="0">
            <Sequence lockVolume="true" lockPitch="true">
                <SetVolume volume="69"/>
                <SetReverb reverb="5"/>
                <SetInstrument wav="menu_show_choice"/>
                <Play pitch="60" velocity="110" length="4"/>
                <Delay ticks="12"/>
                <Play pitch="64" velocity="110" length="4"/>
                <Delay ticks="12"/>
                <Play pitch="67" velocity="110" length="19"/>
                <Delay ticks="48"/>
                <End/>
            </Sequence>
        </Track>
    </Tracks>
</SoundEffect>
```

`Play` starts a note. `pitch` is a note number, `velocity` runs from `0` to `127`, and `length` is in ticks. `Delay` waits for the given number of ticks before the next command. `End` finishes the sequence. The game's sequences in `audio/sfx/` show the other commands, such as `StartLoop`, `EndLoop`, and `PitchSweep`.

To start a sequence, make a sound effect with **New > Sound Effect** and replace its `OneShot` with `Tracks`, or copy one of the game's sequences that behaves like the sound you want, such as `audio/sfx/menu_show_choice.xml`, and change the copy.

A sequence can pick a press envelope with `<SetPressEnvelope ref="envelope03"/>`. Each envelope is a file in `audio/sfx/envelopes/`, named after the envelope.

## Playing the sound

In C:

```c
sfx_play_sound(SOUND_SHOP_BELL);
```

In EVT:

```c
Call(PlaySound, SOUND_SHOP_BELL)
```

## Silence a sound

A `SoundEffect` with no children is silent. To silence one of the game's sounds but keep its constant, remove everything inside its `SoundEffect`. The game's `audio/sfx/hit_silent.xml` is one.
